      "ai": {
        "model": "bai/qwen3.8-flash",
        "v": 5,
        "title": "Structured tool messages and includeToolCall flag",
        "summary": "Agent message types migrated from loose string arrays to Zod-validated structured objects, introducing a dedicated `tool` role with `toolName`, `toolCallId`, and `output` fields. A new `includeToolCall` boolean flag added to `ToolCall` types allows suppressing tool invocation records in message history. Context pruner and planner agents updated to handle the new object format, replacing string-based content checks with typed property access. Core agent definitions now import `Message` from new `codebuff-message.ts` schema file instead of defining it inline.",
        "significance": "notable",
        "at": "2026-09-15T10:08:47.067Z"
      },
      "eli5": {
        "text": "When an AI helper uses a tool (like reading files or running a search) and gets a result back, that result is now saved in a clear, structured format with labeled fields for tool name, unique call ID, and output, instead of as a loose piece of text. A new setting lets the system quietly skip recording these tool usage notes in the message history when it’s not useful to keep them, like when a background check or summary step happens, keeping the conversation thread cleaner. If you looked, you’d see that messages from tools now show up as a distinct “tool” role rather than mixed in with regular text, and some internal helper agents that organize or clean up the work no longer leave behind noisy tool call records. This affects the AI agents that work behind the scenes on your code changes,",
        "model": "vyce/deepseek-v4.1",
        "v": 4,
        "src": "69fe119bb99c",
        "at": "2026-09-17T21:03:41.099Z"
      },
